1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,323
One password in the wrong place can cost a company its whole cloud account.

2
00:00:05,323 --> 00:00:08,024
Bots scan public repositories for secrets,

3
00:00:08,024 --> 00:00:09,375
often within minutes.

4
00:00:09,925 --> 00:00:12,414
Today you learn how secrets leak into Git,

5
00:00:12,414 --> 00:00:14,251
what to do the moment one does,

6
00:00:14,251 --> 00:00:17,925
and how to give people and programs only the access they need.

7
00:00:18,475 --> 00:00:24,000
You will use a leaked password, an S S H key, an access token, and repository roles.

8
00:00:24,550 --> 00:00:27,366
As always, there is a lab with a checker at the end.

9
00:00:29,416 --> 00:00:33,253
A secret is anything that proves who you are: a password,

10
00:00:33,253 --> 00:00:33,994
an API key,

11
00:00:33,994 --> 00:00:34,532
a token,

12
00:00:34,532 --> 00:00:35,475
a private key.

13
00:00:36,025 --> 00:00:37,140
Git never forgets.

14
00:00:37,140 --> 00:00:38,814
Once a secret is committed,

15
00:00:38,814 --> 00:00:40,301
it lives in the history,

16
00:00:40,301 --> 00:00:43,214
even if you delete the file in the next commit.

17
00:00:43,764 --> 00:00:45,702
Rule one: keep secrets out.

18
00:00:45,702 --> 00:00:47,568
Use environment variables,

19
00:00:47,568 --> 00:00:50,008
a dot E N V file that Git ignores,

20
00:00:50,008 --> 00:00:51,444
or a secret manager.

21
00:00:51,994 --> 00:00:53,477
Rule two: if one leaks,

22
00:00:53,477 --> 00:00:54,766
assume it is stolen.

23
00:00:54,766 --> 00:00:55,798
Rotate it first,

24
00:00:55,798 --> 00:00:58,893
which means make a new one and kill the old one.

25
00:00:58,893 --> 00:01:00,698
Only then clean the history.

26
00:01:01,248 --> 00:01:02,757
Rule three: least access.

27
00:01:02,757 --> 00:01:06,923
An S S H key pair has a private half that never leaves your computer,

28
00:01:06,923 --> 00:01:08,613
and a public half you share.

29
00:01:08,613 --> 00:01:10,907
Tokens should have the smallest scope,

30
00:01:10,907 --> 00:01:12,960
and roles the smallest permission.

31
00:01:15,010 --> 00:01:20,130
A real mistake, live. A small app repository. Go in. Type: CD, app.

32
00:01:20,680 --> 00:01:24,435
Look at the history. Type: git log, dash dash one line.

33
00:01:24,985 --> 00:01:30,659
Add database settings. That sounds risky. Look at the file. Type: cat, dot E N V.

34
00:01:31,209 --> 00:01:32,592
A database password,

35
00:01:32,592 --> 00:01:33,283
committed.

36
00:01:33,283 --> 00:01:34,459
This one is fake,

37
00:01:34,459 --> 00:01:36,118
but the mistake is real.

38
00:01:36,118 --> 00:01:37,224
First rotate it,

39
00:01:37,224 --> 00:01:38,123
in real life.

40
00:01:38,123 --> 00:01:40,058
Then stop tracking the file.

41
00:01:40,058 --> 00:01:40,819
Type: echo,

42
00:01:40,819 --> 00:01:41,234
quote,

43
00:01:41,234 --> 00:01:41,925
dot E N V,

44
00:01:41,925 --> 00:01:42,340
quote,

45
00:01:42,340 --> 00:01:43,100
two arrows,

46
00:01:43,100 --> 00:01:44,137
dot git ignore.

47
00:01:44,687 --> 00:01:51,194
Remove it from Git, but keep the file on disk. Type: git R M, dash dash cached, dot E N V.

48
00:01:51,744 --> 00:01:55,371
Stage the ignore file. Type: git add, dot git ignore.

49
00:01:55,921 --> 00:02:00,550
Commit. Type: git commit, dash M, quote, Stop tracking dot E N V, quote.

50
00:02:01,100 --> 00:02:01,516
Fixed?

51
00:02:01,516 --> 00:02:04,426
Search the whole history for the password.

52
00:02:04,426 --> 00:02:05,396
Type: git log,

53
00:02:05,396 --> 00:02:05,880
dash S,

54
00:02:05,880 --> 00:02:07,058
demo dash secret,

55
00:02:07,058 --> 00:02:08,375
dash dash one line.

56
00:02:08,925 --> 00:02:09,633
Still there!

57
00:02:09,633 --> 00:02:11,875
The old commit still holds the secret,

58
00:02:11,875 --> 00:02:13,940
and anyone with a copy can read it.

59
00:02:13,940 --> 00:02:15,887
This is why rotating comes first.

60
00:02:15,887 --> 00:02:18,129
Now rewrite history without that file.

61
00:02:18,129 --> 00:02:19,723
Type: git filter dash repo,

62
00:02:19,723 --> 00:02:20,608
dash dash path,

63
00:02:20,608 --> 00:02:21,198
dot E N V,

64
00:02:21,198 --> 00:02:22,850
dash dash invert dash paths,

65
00:02:22,850 --> 00:02:23,794
dash dash force.

66
00:02:24,344 --> 00:02:29,635
Search again. Type: git log, dash S, demo dash secret, dash dash one line.

67
00:02:30,185 --> 00:02:34,729
Nothing found. Check the history. Type: git log, dash dash one line.

68
00:02:35,279 --> 00:02:37,063
The secret commit is gone.

69
00:02:37,063 --> 00:02:38,985
On a real shared repository,

70
00:02:38,985 --> 00:02:40,564
everyone must re-clone,

71
00:02:40,564 --> 00:02:43,035
and the old password must stay dead.

72
00:02:43,035 --> 00:02:47,908
The force flag is only safe here because this is a practice repository.

73
00:02:49,958 --> 00:02:53,492
Now S S H keys: log in without a password,

74
00:02:53,492 --> 00:02:54,081
safely.

75
00:02:54,081 --> 00:02:55,428
Make the folder.

76
00:02:55,428 --> 00:02:56,606
Type: M K dir,

77
00:02:56,606 --> 00:02:57,195
dash P,

78
00:02:57,195 --> 00:02:59,046
tilde slash dot S S H.

79
00:02:59,596 --> 00:03:00,932
Generate a key pair.

80
00:03:00,932 --> 00:03:02,601
Type: S S H dash key gen,

81
00:03:02,601 --> 00:03:03,068
dash T,

82
00:03:03,068 --> 00:03:04,871
E D two five five one nine,

83
00:03:04,871 --> 00:03:05,339
dash C,

84
00:03:05,339 --> 00:03:06,875
sam at example dot com,

85
00:03:06,875 --> 00:03:07,342
dash F,

86
00:03:07,342 --> 00:03:11,215
tilde slash dot S S H slash lab sixty-four underscore key,

87
00:03:11,215 --> 00:03:11,683
dash N,

88
00:03:11,683 --> 00:03:12,417
two quotes.

89
00:03:12,967 --> 00:03:17,618
Two files were made. Look. Type: L S, tilde slash dot S S H.

90
00:03:18,168 --> 00:03:21,116
The one ending in dot pub is the public key.

91
00:03:21,116 --> 00:03:23,729
This is the one you paste into git hub.

92
00:03:23,729 --> 00:03:24,398
Type: cat,

93
00:03:24,398 --> 00:03:25,269
the key path,

94
00:03:25,269 --> 00:03:25,805
dot pub.

95
00:03:26,355 --> 00:03:28,339
The other file is the private key.

96
00:03:28,339 --> 00:03:29,214
Never share it,

97
00:03:29,214 --> 00:03:30,147
never commit it.

98
00:03:30,147 --> 00:03:31,780
Peek at its first line only.

99
00:03:31,780 --> 00:03:32,422
Type: head,

100
00:03:32,422 --> 00:03:33,064
dash N one,

101
00:03:33,064 --> 00:03:33,822
the key path.

102
00:03:34,372 --> 00:03:35,153
In real life,

103
00:03:35,153 --> 00:03:37,315
always set a passphrase on your key.

104
00:03:37,315 --> 00:03:39,897
We skipped it only to keep this demo short.

105
00:03:41,947 --> 00:03:43,773
Programs need access too.

106
00:03:43,773 --> 00:03:46,766
On git hub it is a personal access token.

107
00:03:46,766 --> 00:03:48,373
This demo is on Gitea,

108
00:03:48,373 --> 00:03:50,125
which has the same idea.

109
00:03:50,125 --> 00:03:51,513
Open your settings,

110
00:03:51,513 --> 00:03:52,827
then Applications.

111
00:03:58,777 --> 00:04:02,361
Give the token a name that says what it is for: CI read only.

112
00:04:02,911 --> 00:04:03,938
Now the scopes.

113
00:04:03,938 --> 00:04:06,059
Everything starts at No Access.

114
00:04:06,059 --> 00:04:08,797
Give the one thing it needs: repository,

115
00:04:08,797 --> 00:04:09,482
read only.

116
00:04:10,032 --> 00:04:11,161
Generate the token.

117
00:04:11,161 --> 00:04:12,171
It is shown once.

118
00:04:12,171 --> 00:04:14,250
Copy it into your secret store now,

119
00:04:14,250 --> 00:04:16,389
because you will never see it again.

120
00:04:16,939 --> 00:04:19,377
Treat it like a password: never in code,

121
00:04:19,377 --> 00:04:20,352
never in a chat.

122
00:04:20,352 --> 00:04:22,486
And revoke it when the job is done.

123
00:04:24,536 --> 00:04:28,739
Now people. Open the repository, then Settings, then Collaborators.

124
00:04:32,389 --> 00:04:33,322
Alex can write.

125
00:04:33,322 --> 00:04:35,812
That is right for a teammate who pushes.

126
00:04:35,812 --> 00:04:37,679
For a reviewer who only reads,

127
00:04:37,679 --> 00:04:38,426
we use Read.

128
00:04:38,976 --> 00:04:42,987
Change Alex to Read. The menu offers Read, Write, and Admin.

129
00:04:46,337 --> 00:04:49,967
Give people the smallest role that lets them do their job.

130
00:04:49,967 --> 00:04:51,970
Admin can delete the repository,

131
00:04:51,970 --> 00:04:53,910
so keep it for very few people.

132
00:04:55,960 --> 00:04:58,733
Your task, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

133
00:04:59,283 --> 00:05:01,776
The lab is lab sixty-four dot S H,

134
00:05:01,776 --> 00:05:05,516
in the academy slash lab folder of git hub dot com,

135
00:05:05,516 --> 00:05:06,982
slash it dash waves,

136
00:05:06,982 --> 00:05:08,669
slash dev ops dash A I.

137
00:05:08,669 --> 00:05:10,355
Install it like before,

138
00:05:10,355 --> 00:05:11,529
then run lab up.

139
00:05:12,079 --> 00:05:16,111
It builds a repository where a fake password was committed by mistake.

140
00:05:16,661 --> 00:05:20,693
One: stop tracking dot E N V, and list it in dot git ignore.

141
00:05:21,243 --> 00:05:23,867
Two: rewrite the history so the secret is gone.

142
00:05:24,417 --> 00:05:31,009
Three: make an E D two five five one nine S S H key named lab sixty-four underscore key.

143
00:05:31,559 --> 00:05:33,132
Four: in answers dot text,

144
00:05:33,132 --> 00:05:37,127
write the first thing you do when a secret leaks: the word rotate.

145
00:05:37,677 --> 00:05:41,367
Then run lab check. Four pass lines. Rotate, then clean.

146
00:05:43,417 --> 00:05:45,401
Six points, and you need all six.

147
00:05:45,951 --> 00:05:47,786
The checker shows four pass lines.

148
00:05:48,336 --> 00:05:50,362
The secret is gone from every commit.

149
00:05:50,912 --> 00:05:53,920
Your private key stays private, and is never committed.

150
00:05:54,470 --> 00:05:57,521
You can explain why rotate comes before cleaning history.

151
00:05:58,071 --> 00:06:01,399
You can name the smallest token scope or role for a job.

152
00:06:01,949 --> 00:06:03,592
You ran lab down to clean up.

153
00:06:04,142 --> 00:06:05,763
To pass, you need all six.

154
00:06:07,813 --> 00:06:10,650
Your turn, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

155
00:06:11,200 --> 00:06:13,419
One: install the lab, and run lab up.

156
00:06:13,969 --> 00:06:16,252
Two: ignore and untrack dot E N V.

157
00:06:16,802 --> 00:06:19,447
Three: rewrite history with git filter repo.

158
00:06:19,997 --> 00:06:22,152
Four: generate the S S H key.

159
00:06:22,702 --> 00:06:25,262
Five: write the answer, and run lab check.

160
00:06:25,812 --> 00:06:27,284
Six: run lab down.

161
00:06:27,834 --> 00:06:29,668
Pause the video, and begin now.

162
00:06:45,218 --> 00:06:46,093
Quick check.

163
00:06:46,643 --> 00:06:49,949
Why does deleting a file not remove a secret from Git?

164
00:06:50,499 --> 00:06:52,569
What do you do first when a secret leaks?

165
00:06:53,119 --> 00:06:55,721
Which half of an S S H key pair do you share?

166
00:06:56,271 --> 00:06:57,466
What is least access?

167
00:06:58,016 --> 00:07:00,618
Objective covered: M C zero seven,

168
00:07:00,618 --> 00:07:01,842
objective eight,

169
00:07:01,842 --> 00:07:02,454
secrets,

170
00:07:02,454 --> 00:07:03,373
credentials,

171
00:07:03,373 --> 00:07:05,056
and repository access.

172
00:07:05,606 --> 00:07:07,675
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

173
00:07:18,225 --> 00:07:21,340
Next: a team release workflow with automated checks.

174
00:07:21,890 --> 00:07:25,218
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
